      "code_path": "sui-move-contract/contracts/protocol/sources/internal/market/market.move::liquidate_calculate_seize_ctokens::L1031-1074",
      "proof_trace": "`liquidate_calculate_seize_ctokens` uses `get_spot_price` (Pyth spot) for both `price_borrowed` and `price_collateral` (L1045-1046), while the upstream liquidation eligibility check in `ensure_liquidate_borrow_allowed` uses `get_price` (Pyth EMA) via `collaterals_usd_for_liquidation` and `debts_value_usd_for_liquidation`. When spot diverges from EMA during market volatility (e.g., collateral spot < collateral EMA during a crash), the seize formula yields more cTokens than the EMA-consistent amount, extracting excess collateral from borrowers. No design comment or code rationale explains this asymmetric price-source selection; `get_price` and `get_price_with_check` are both imported in the same file but unused here.",
      "attack_scenario": "During a fast collateral price drop (spot falls ahead of EMA \u2014 the typical crash pattern), a liquidator submits a liquidation transaction: the EMA-based eligibility check passes (position is legitimately underwater at EMA prices), but the seize calculation uses the lower spot price for `price_collateral`, yielding proportionally more seized cTokens per unit of debt repaid than the protocol intends, extracting the price-divergence delta as profit at the borrower's expense.",
      "checklist_id": "PROP-01b-partial3-inv-015"
    },
    "text": "seizeTokens calculation must correctly include liquidation incentive and oracle prices.",
    "assertion": "seizeTokens == actualRepay * liqIncentive * priceBorrow / (priceCollateral * exchangeRateCollateral)",
    "covers": "SG-005",
    "severity": "HIGH",
    "type": "invariant"
  }
}

